{title: A Mighty Fortress Is Our God}
{artist: Martin Luther (1529); tr. Frederick H. Hedge (1853)}
{key: C}
{tempo: 88}
{time: 4/4}
{meta: tune EIN FESTE BURG - Martin Luther (1529)}
{meta: public_domain Text 1853, tune 1529: both published before 1931 (US public domain); all authors, translators and composers died over 70 years ago. Chords and section layout: original simple arrangement by Tabernacle Tools. Original verses only, no modern additions.}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
A [C]mighty fortress [G]is our [C]God, a [F]bulwark [G]never [C]failing;
Our [C]helper He, a[G]mid the [C]flood of [F]mortal [G]ills pre[C]vailing:
For [Am]still our ancient [E]foe doth [Am]seek to work us [G]woe;
His [C]craft and power are [F]great, and, [Dm]armed with [G]cruel [C]hate,
On [F]earth is [C]not his [G]equal.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
Did [C]we in our own [G]strength con[C]fide, our [F]striving [G]would be [C]losing;
Were [C]not the right Man [G]on our [C]side, the [F]Man of [G]God's own [C]choosing:
Dost [Am]ask who that may [E]be? Christ [Am]Jesus, it is [G]He;
Lord [C]Sabaoth His [F]name, from [Dm]age to [G]age the [C]same,
And [F]He must [C]win the [G]bat[C]tle.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
And [C]though this world, with [G]devils [C]filled, should [F]threaten [G]to un[C]do us,
We [C]will not fear, for [G]God hath [C]willed His [F]truth to [G]triumph [C]through us:
The [Am]Prince of Darkness [E]grim, we [Am]tremble not for [G]him;
His [C]rage we can en[F]dure, for [Dm]lo, his [G]doom is [C]sure,
One [F]little [C]word shall [G]fell [C]him.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
That [C]word above all [G]earthly [C]powers, no [F]thanks to [G]them, a[C]bideth;
The [C]Spirit and the [G]gifts are [C]ours through [F]Him who [G]with us [C]sideth:
Let [Am]goods and kindred [E]go, this [Am]mortal life [G]also;
The [C]body they may [F]kill: God's [Dm]truth a[G]bideth [C]still,
His [F]kingdom [C]is for[G]ev[C]er.
{end_of_verse}
